answer.worked:
The graph $y=\sqrt{4-x^2}$ is the upper semicircle with radius $2$ and centre $(0,0)$.

At $x=1$, $y=\sqrt{3}$. The required area is the sum of a triangle and a sector of angle $\frac{\pi}{6}$.
$$\begin{align*}
\int_0^1\sqrt{4-x^2}\,dx &= \frac{1}{2}(1)(\sqrt{3})+\frac{1}{2}(2)^2\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right) \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}+\frac{\pi}{3}
\end{align*}$$

